<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lewis and Clark Expedition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Corps of Discovery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lewis and Clark Expedition was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army expedition led by Meriwether Lewis and William Clark from 1804 to 1806 to explore the newly acquired Louisiana Purchase and find a route to the Pacific. Aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshone">Shoshone</a> woman Sacagawea, it travelled up the Missouri River and across the Rocky Mountains to the Pacific coast and back, recording the land and its peoples, plants and animals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lewis-and-clark-expedition.jpg" alt="Lewis and Clark Expedition" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lewis_and_Clark.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the <b>Lewis and Clark Expedition</b> met the Sicangu in 1804, warriors seized a boat’s cable and both sides readied their weapons, until the Sicangu leader Black Buffalo let go and invited the Americans upriver under Sicangu escort.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
